{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J7tNZ3PNrB5Q8mbXSMEUJi","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"C:/Users/dev/AppData/Local/Temp/record-run-5943775966978768415/plan.json","content":"{
  "type": "set_plan",
  "title": "McpEndpoint: the set_plan tool spoken as MCP",
  "language": "en",
  "frames": [
    {
      "id": 0,
      "file": "C:/_code/JetbrainsAudioExplainer/src/main/kotlin/com/audioexplainer/McpEndpoint.kt",
      "title": "McpEndpoint",
      "explanation": "A minimal MCP server over Streamable HTTP with plain JSON replies. It exposes one tool, `set_plan`, so a headless run hands its plan to the plugin as a tool call instead of writing a file and POSTing it. `handle` answers one JSON-RPC request; `config` writes the run's `--mcp-config` document.",
      "keep": [
        { "start": 32, "end": 37, "role": "context" },
        { "start": 76, "end": 118, "role": "focus" },
        { "start": 124, "end": 132, "role": "context" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "frame": 0,
      "ranges": [ { "start": 32, "end": 37 } ],
      "ttsText": "McpEndpoint is a tiny MCP server with exactly one tool, set plan. Its qualified name, the one Claude Code's allow list grants, is built from the server name and the tool name. The tool's input schema is deliberately loose, because the real schema lives in the skill's docs and the plugin's loader reports on a plan rather than refusing it.",
      "explanation": "One tool, one name"
    },
    {
      "frame": 0,
      "ranges": [ { "start": 77, "end": 84 } ],
      "ttsText": "Handle takes one HTTP request. Anything but a POST gets 405, telling the client there is no event stream to open. The body must parse as a single JSON-RPC message, and a message without an id is a notification, answered with 202 and no body.",
      "explanation": "Request gatekeeping"
    },
    {
      "frame": 0,
      "ranges": [ { "start": 95, "end": 109 } ],
      "ttsText": "Initialize, ping and tools list get fixed answers. The interesting branch is tools call: it checks the tool name, stamps the arguments with type set plan, and passes them to the setPlan callback, which the server owns. Whatever text comes back becomes the tool result the agent reads, flagged as an error when the plan was invalid.",
      "explanation": "tools/call dispatch"
    },
    {
      "frame": 0,
      "ranges": [ { "start": 124, "end": 132 } ],
      "ttsText": "Finally, config builds the MCP config document for one run: just this server, over HTTP, with the run's own bearer token in a header. It goes into a file rather than a command line argument, so other local processes cannot read the token.",
      "explanation": "Per-run MCP config"
    }
  ]
}
"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null},"request_id":"req_011CgmpdjrvxbnJ6yMjWSYW7","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01J7tNZ3PNrB5Q8mbXSMEUJi":{"file_path":"C:/Users/dev/AppData/Local/Temp/record-run-5943775966978768415/plan.json","content":"{
